      "problem": "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{2}⁡(x+2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246608/assets/5c4b062938cc4e3d64f11f9ad3bd8da9431581e9a1b0278a977a64c0d727c73d.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-2.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}{log}_{2}⁡(x+2)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$",
